   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# K-Means Algorithm\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8e58ed34",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "K-Means is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm used for clustering data into K distinct groups. The algorithm works by assigning each data point to the nearest cluster centroid, then updating the centroids iteratively until convergence. Its objective is to minimize the distance between data points and their respective cluster centers, making points within the same cluster more similar to each other. K-Means is widely used for data analysis, customer segmentation, recommendation systems, and pattern discovery.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b5c5af69",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 1.Key Concepts\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "61317efb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Understanding the fundamental concepts of K-Means is important before exploring its implementation. These concepts explain how the algorithm groups data and optimizes cluster formation.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Cluster\n",
    "\n",
    "A **cluster** is a collection of data points that share similar characteristics.\n",
    "\n",
    "The objective of K-Means is to group similar observations into the same cluster while keeping different observations in separate clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, in customer segmentation, people with similar purchasing habits may belong to the same cluster.\n",
    "\n",
    "A good cluster generally contains:\n",
    "- highly similar points within the cluster,\n",
    "- clear separation from other clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Centroid\n",
    "\n",
    "A **centroid** represents the center of a cluster.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is computed as the mean position of all data points belonging to that cluster.\n",
    "\n",
    "During training, K-Means continuously updates centroid positions in order to better represent the structure of the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Each data point is assigned to the cluster whose centroid is closest to it.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Iteration\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means is an **iterative algorithm**, meaning it repeatedly performs a sequence of operations until stability is reached.\n",
    "\n",
    "At each iteration, the algorithm:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Assigns each data point to the nearest centroid.\n",
    "2. Recalculates centroid positions using the mean of assigned points.\n",
    "\n",
    "This process gradually improves cluster quality.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Convergence\n",
    "\n",
    "**Convergence** occurs when centroid positions stop changing significantly between iterations.\n",
    "\n",
    "At this stage, the algorithm assumes that an optimal or near-optimal clustering structure has been found and stops execution.\n",
    "\n",
    "Convergence may also occur when the maximum number of iterations is reached."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "78059408",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 2-Distance Metrics\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5759f4b4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Distance metrics are mathematical measures used to evaluate the similarity or dissimilarity between data points.\n",
    "\n",
    "In clustering, distance plays a fundamental role because K-Means groups points based on proximity. Data points that are closer together are more likely to belong to the same cluster.\n",
    "\n",
    "The most commonly used distance metric in K-Means is the **Euclidean Distance**, since the algorithm minimizes the squared distance between data points and their centroids.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.1 Euclidean Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "Euclidean distance measures the straight-line distance between two points in space.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is the default distance metric used in traditional K-Means because centroids are computed using the arithmetic mean.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Euclidean distance between two points is defined as:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d(x,y)=\\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i-y_i)^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) represent two data points,\n",
    "- \\(n\\) is the number of features,\n",
    "- \\(x_i\\) and \\(y_i\\) are feature values.\n",
    "\n",
    "A smaller Euclidean distance indicates that two points are more similar.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.2 Manhattan Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Manhattan Distance** measures the distance between two points by summing the absolute differences across dimensions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of a straight line, it follows a grid-like path (similar to moving through city streets).\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d(x,y)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_i-y_i|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This metric is useful when:\n",
    "- data contains outliers,\n",
    "- movement is constrained to horizontal and vertical directions,\n",
    "- absolute differences are more meaningful than squared differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.3 Minkowski Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Minkowski Distance** is a generalized distance metric that includes both Euclidean and Manhattan distances as special cases.\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d(x,y)=\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_i-y_i|^p\\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where \\(p\\) determines the type of distance:\n",
    "\n",
    "- \\(p=1\\) → Manhattan Distance  \n",
    "- \\(p=2\\) → Euclidean Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "Minkowski distance provides flexibility for adapting to different data structures.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.4 Cosine Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Cosine Distance** measures similarity based on the angle between vectors rather than physical distance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of measuring magnitude differences, it evaluates direction similarity.\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Cosine Similarity}=\n",
    "\\frac{x \\cdot y}{||x|| \\ ||y||}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This metric is commonly used for:\n",
    "- text mining,\n",
    "- document clustering,\n",
    "- recommendation systems.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2.5 Hamming Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Hamming Distance** measures the number of positions where two values differ.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is mostly used for:\n",
    "- binary data,\n",
    "- categorical variables,\n",
    "- encoded text comparisons.\n",
    "\n",
    "Unlike Euclidean distance, it is not suitable for continuous numerical data.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why Does K-Means Prefer Euclidean Distance?\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means is specifically designed to minimize squared Euclidean distances between points and centroids.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because centroids are computed using arithmetic means, Euclidean distance naturally aligns with the optimization objective of the algorithm.\n",
    "\n",
    "Using alternative distance metrics may require modified clustering algorithms such as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **K-Medoids**,\n",
    "- **K-Modes**,\n",
    "- **Kernel K-Means**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, although other distance metrics exist, Euclidean distance remains the most appropriate and widely used choice for standard K-Means clustering.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c435f487",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3. Pseudo Algorithm\n",
    "\n",
    "The K-Means algorithm follows these steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Choose the number of clusters K\n",
    "2. Initialize K centroids\n",
    "3. Assign each point to the nearest centroid\n",
    "4. Update centroids using cluster means\n",
    "5. Repeat until convergence"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c17fb127",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Objective Function"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9ffb91d2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "$$\n",
    "J=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sum_{x_j\\in C_i}||x_j-\\mu_i||^2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "020f0cdb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- \\( J \\) : Objective function minimized by K-Means\n",
    "- \\( k \\) : Number of clusters\n",
    "- \\( Ci \\) : Set of points belonging to cluster \\( i \\)\n",
    "- \\( xj \\) : A data point\n",
    "- \\( µi \\) : Centroid of cluster \\( i \\)\n",
    "- \\( ||xj - µi||^2 \\) : Squared Euclidean distance between a point and its centroid"
   ]
  },
